          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The flavor of (classical)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            called
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S4
            is (classical)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
            equipped with a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            usually written “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            ” subject to the rules that for all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p, q \colon Prop
            we have
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo □
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo □
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mo □
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box K \colon \Box(p \to q) \to (\Box p \to \Box q)
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K+modal+logic"}> K modal logic
                )
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo □
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box T \colon \Box p \to p
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T+modal+logic"}> T modal logic
                )
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo □
                      %mn 4
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo □
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mo □
                      %mo □
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box 4 \colon \Box p \to \Box \Box p
                \. (S4 modal logic)
            %li
              %p
                (in addition in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S5+modal+logic"} S5 modal logic
                one adds:
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ◊
                        %mi p
                        %mo →
                        %mo □
                        %mo ◊
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lozenge p \to \Box \lozenge p
          %p
            Traditionally the canonical interpretation of the Box operator is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box p
            is the statement that “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessarily
            true.” (The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+duality"} de Morgan dual
            to the necessity modality is the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"}> possibility
            ” modality.) Then the interpretation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box 4
            is that “If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is necessarily true then it is necessarily necessarily true.” S4 modal logic appears in many
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temporal+logics"} temporal logics
          %p
            If instead of a single Box operator one considers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            box operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_i
            of this form, the resulting modal logic is denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mn 4
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S4(n)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mi i
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_i p
            is sometimes interpreted as “the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            In intuitionistic (or constructive) Modal Logic, one does not expect the possibility modality to be dual to the necessity modality, necessarily.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Simpson"} Alex Simpson
            has written in his thesis the most used constructive modal logic system, but other systems exist.
